WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.420
Imagine if we had an accurate algorithm to
simulate how different sound effects would

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.680
propagate in a virtual world.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.510
We would find computer games exhibiting gunfire
in open areas, or a pianist inside a castle

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:21.990
courtyard to be way more immersive, and we've
been waiting for efficient techniques for

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.860
this for quite a while now.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.160
This is a research field where convolutions
enjoy quite a bit of attention due to the

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.430
fact that they are a reliable and efficient
way to approximate how a given signal would

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.399
sound in a room with given geometry and material
properties.

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:43.559
However, the keyword is approximate - this,
however, is one of those path sampling techniques

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:47.270
that gives us the real deal, so quite excited
for that!

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.520
So what about this path sampling thing?

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.809
This means an actual simulation of sound waves.

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:59.149
We have a vast literature and decades of experience
in simulating how rays of light bounce and

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:05.059
reflect around in a scene, and leaning on
this knowledge, we can create beautiful photorealistic

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:06.070
images.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:11.319
The first idea is to adapt the mathematical
framework of light simulations to be able

00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:14.380
to do the very same with sound waves.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.810
Path tracing is a technique where we build
light paths from the camera, bounce them around

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.499
in a scene, and hope that we hit a light source
with these rays.

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:26.859
If this happens, then we compute the amount
of energy that is transferred from the light

00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:28.389
source to the camera.

00:01:28.389 --> 00:01:33.869
Note that energy is a more popular and journalistic
term here, what researchers actually measure

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:36.950
here is a quantity called radiance.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:42.329
The main contribution of this work is adapting
bidirectional path tracing to sound.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:46.889
This is a technique originally designed for
light simulations that builds light paths

00:01:46.889 --> 00:01:52.179
from both the light source and the camera
at the same time, and it is significantly

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:56.810
more efficient than the classical path tracer
on difficult indoors scenes.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.599
And of course, the main issue with these methods
is that they have to simulate a large number

00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:06.509
of rays to obtain a satisfactory result, and
many of these rays don't really contribute

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.849
anything to the final result, only a small
subset of them are responsible for most of

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:14.709
the image we see or sound we hear.

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:37.920
It is a bit like the Pareto principle or the
80/20 rule 

00:02:37.920 --> 00:03:30.560
on steroids.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.719
This is ice cream for my ears.

00:03:32.719 --> 00:03:33.749
Love it!

00:03:33.749 --> 00:03:39.109
This work also introduces a metric to not
only be able to compare similar sound synthesis

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:43.560
techniques in the future, but the proposed
technique is built around minimizing this

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.640
metric, which leads us to an idea on which
rays carry important information and which

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.569
ones we are better off discarding.

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:56.420
I also like this minimap on the upper left
that actually shows what we hear in this footage,

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.590
exactly where the sound sources are and how
they change their positions.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:05.329
Looking forward to seeing and listening to
similar presentations in future papers in

00:04:05.329 --> 00:04:06.329
this area!

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:11.379
A typical number for the execution time of
the algorithm is between 15-20 milliseconds

00:04:11.379 --> 00:04:14.150
per frame on a consumer-grade processor.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.560
That is about 50-65 frames per second.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.440
The position of the sound sources makes a
great deal of difference for the classical

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.680
path tracer.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:28.150
The bidirectional path tracer, however, is
not only more effective, but offers significantly

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.389
more consistent results as well.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:34.000
This new method is especially useful in these
cases.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.590
There are way more details explained in the
paper, for instance, it also supports path

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:44.060
caching and also borrows the all-powerful
multiple importance sampling from photorealistic

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.479
rendering research.

00:04:45.479 --> 00:04:46.479
Have a look!

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:49.740
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

